# sdin: an API-first configuration language

Most config languages focus on configuration authors, not the programmers working with configuration objects at the AST level.

Say you want to sort a specified list field's members.
The AST APIs for most languages either hurt your head or are so simplistic that they strip comments and reformat the whole file.
Here's an sdin struct before and after such sorting:

```split
# Before sorting.
developers: {
	Data.Members: [
		'Charlie'
		'Frank'    # intern, until the end of year
		'Alice'
	]
}
---
# After sorting.
developers: {
	Data.Members: [
		'Alice'
		'Charlie'
		'Frank'    # intern, until the end of year
	]
}
```

Notice how Frank's comment remained correctly attached.
The code for this is only a few lines of Go that you can find in the ``#Nodes1|#Nodes1`` section.

This page describes sdin, a new configuration language whose primary focus is a simple AST API, achieved by limiting the syntax.
For example, the struct field names must be identifiers, there is only one type of comment, the field name and value must be on the same line, etc.
I started from JSON and added/removed features for human convenience, but only those that didn't add unnecessary complexity to its API.

I built it for my @/sdinthrough coding challenge as an educational language, which was the motivation for keeping the implementation simple.
I ended up liking it and now use it everywhere I can (also known as Stockholm syndrome).

This is pretty much the full reference for sdin, so it's long and dense.
It has a lot of Go code samples because the API is where this language's focus lies; that's what I want to showcase.

