## Full example

sdin is Turing-incomplete; it just describes a tree-structured object.
As a sneak peek, here's what a complex config from the coding challenge looks like:

```
#% stdemo.SkipJSONEmit()
Vars: {
	HealthCheck: {Path:'/health' Interval:30s Timeout:5s Retries:3}
	RetryPolicy: {MaxAttempts:5 Backoff:'exponential'}
}

Title: 'High Availability redundant setup'

Clusters: {
	# Expanded, JSON-style:
	Primary: {
		Name:        'us-east-1-prod'
		Endpoints:   ['10.0.1.5:8080' '10.0.1.6:8080']
		HealthCheck: stdemo.Var(HealthCheck)
		RetryPolicy: stdemo.Var(RetryPolicy)
	}

	# Flat, sdin-style:
	Secondary.Name:        'us-west-2-prod'
	Secondary.Endpoints+:  '10.0.2.5:8080'
	Secondary.Endpoints+:  '10.0.2.6:8080'
	Secondary.HealthCheck: stdemo.Var(HealthCheck)
	Secondary.RetryPolicy: stdemo.Var(RetryPolicy)
}
```

## FAQ: Foremost Anticipated Questions

> Why yet another configuration language?

I wanted a nice configuration language as the subject of my @/sdinthrough coding challenge.
None of the existing ones had the exact qualities I was looking for in this educational setting, so it was easier for me to create my own rather than degrade the challenge with details I didn't want to include.
And for a long time, I've been wanting to create my own language as an exercise, too.

> Other than its API and format-preserving manipulation, is there anything else interesting in this language?

``#Forms|Forms``, which are a Lisp-like chimera of structs (named values) and lists (nameless values).
Structurally they are similar to ``https://kdl.dev|KDL`` nodes, but forms are more generic and have a different syntax.

> Is sdin only for configuration?

No.
sdin itself just describes a tree, and the parser gives you an AST to process however you like.
That makes it a good building block for DSLs; with forms you can even implement a simple programming language on top of it, though that's not its intended purpose.

> What is the biggest downside of sdin?

It's up to the application that's processing sdin to give meaning to bare tokens and forms.
It's semantically similar to XML, where the meaning is imbued externally too.
This makes portability a big issue, but in exchange it provides great flexibility if an sdin config has a single consumer only.

> Does sdin suffer from the ``https://langdev.stackexchange.com/questions/1123|Norway problem``?

No, unless you make the `no`, `false`, `0`, and `"false"` tokens all mean the same thing.

> My $LANGUAGE has a very nice AST library (hclwrite, tomlkit, toml_edit, ruamel.yaml, etc.).
> What's wrong with it?

All of them have rough edges to deal with the language's idiosyncrasies because they appeared after the language's spec.
In sdin, the syntax was molded such that its Go API is clean.

> Does sdin have broad ecosystem support?

No.
It's my hobby project, and I only have a Go implementation.
But I do have an extensive test suite for implementors at @/sdinthrough.

> What does `sdin` stand for?

Dunno.
Let's go with Structured Data Input Notation.

> How can I try it?

Use my Go package from https://pkg.go.dev/github.com/ypsu/sdin.
Or you can try running the formatter and see what parses:

```
$ cat hello.sdin
Greeting:'Hello'
Target:'World'
$ go run github.com/ypsu/sdin/cmd/sdin@latest format <hello.sdin
Greeting: 'Hello'
Target:   'World'
```

## Basic syntax

sdin removes some fluff from JSON, then re-adds other fluff.
Let me rush through the basic syntax so that it doesn't get in the way later.

Enclosing braces?
Gone.
sdin supports parsing implicit structs.

```split
¹{¹
¹	¹"StringField": "StringValue",
¹	¹"ListField": [1, 2, 3],
¹	¹"Number": 3
¹}¹
---

"StringField": "StringValue",
"ListField": [1, 2, 3],
"Number": 3

```

Quotes around struct fields?
Gone.

```split
¹"¹StringField¹"¹: "StringValue",
¹"¹ListField¹"¹: [1, 2, 3],
¹"¹Number¹"¹: 3
---
StringField: "StringValue",
ListField: [1, 2, 3],
Number: 3
```

Commas?
Gone.

```split
StringField: "StringValue"¹,¹
ListField: [1¹,¹ 2¹,¹ 3]¹,¹
Number: 3
---
StringField: "StringValue"
ListField: [1 2 3]
Number: 3
```

I'm providing a formatter tool too because I'm a sucker for visually aligned columns:

```split
# Unformatted sdin.
StringField: "StringValue"
ListField: [1 2 3]
Number: 3
---
# Formatted sdin.
StringField: "StringValue"
ListField: ²  ²[1 2 3]
Number: ²     ²3
```

Unlike in JSON, sdin allows comments:

```split

StringField: "StringValue"
ListField: [1 2 3]
Number: 3
---
²# Comments begin with the # symbol.²
StringField: "StringValue"  ²# Comments can follow values.²
ListField:   [1 2 3]        ²# The formatter aligns them too.²
Number:      3
```

Unlike in JSON, values can be "bare tokens", which are semantically different from strings.
A bare token is a run of non-reserved symbols (anything except whitespace, `:`, `#`, `'`, `"`, `{`, `}`, `[`, `]`, `(`, `)`).
sdin doesn't have the concept of booleans (or numbers, even); it works at a lower abstraction layer, but more on this in the tokens section.

```
# These values are all different.
Boolean: false
Country: no
String:  'no'

# You can use any non-reserved symbol in bare tokens.
Date:  2026-12-30
Email: alice@example.com

# This list has two elements:
FileList: [/usr/bin/true /usr/bin/false]
```

sdin supports the `+` field annotation for specifying lists succinctly, similarly to repeated fields in textprotos.
It's just a bit of syntactic sugar because I find long lists of structs confusing to read, especially if the list spans more than a screen's worth of lines.

```split
Album: {
	Artist: "The Beatles"
	Title:  "Let It Be"
	Tracks: [
		{
			Title:    "Two of Us"
			Duration: "3m36s"
		}
		{
			Title:    "Dig a Pony"
			Duration: "3m54s"
		}
	]
}
---
Album: {
	Artist: "The Beatles"
	Title:  "Let It Be"
	Tracks²+²: {
		Title:    "Two of Us"
		Duration: "3m36s"
	}
	Tracks²+²: {
		Title:    "Dig a Pony"
		Duration: "3m54s"
	}
}
```

And there's a dot notation for deeply nested structures:

```split
Album: {
	Artist: "The Beatles"
	Title:  "Let It Be"
	ReleaseDate: {
		Year:  1970
		Month: May
		Day:   8
	}
	Tracks+: {
		Title:    "Two of Us"
		Duration: "3m36s"
	}
	Tracks+: {
		Title:    "Dig a Pony"
		Duration: "3m54s"
	}
}
---
Album.Artist:            "The Beatles"
Album.Title:             "Let It Be"
Album.ReleaseDate.Year:  1970
Album.ReleaseDate.Month: May
Album.ReleaseDate.Day:   8
Album.Tracks+: {
	Title:    "Two of Us"
	Duration: "3m36s"
}
Album.Tracks+: {
	Title:    "Dig a Pony"
	Duration: "3m54s"
}
```

Note that both the flat lists and flat structs must be contiguous; otherwise they wouldn't be pure syntactic sugar.
The following two structs are invalid:

```split
$ cat flat_list.sdin
ExampleStruct: {
	ListField+:  1
	StringField: "x"
¹	ListField+:  2
}

$ sdin format flat_list.sdin
sdin.ParseFile file=flat_list.sdin: sdin.RedefinedFlatField line=4 name="ListField" prevline=2
Error: sdin.FileFormattingFailed files=[flat_list.sdin]
---
$ cat flat_struct.sdin
ExampleStruct: {
	StructField.Number1:  1
	OtherNumber:          3
¹	StructField.Number2:  2
}

$ sdin format flat_struct.sdin
sdin.ParseFile file=flat_struct.sdin: sdin.RedefinedFlatField line=4 name="StructField" prevline=2
Error: sdin.FileFormattingFailed files=[flat_struct.sdin]
```

The flat struct syntax is the primary reason that the field names must be identifiers in structs and cannot contain symbols.
Otherwise parsing a line like `A.1.2: B` would get complicated very fast.
Is it representing `A:{1:{2:B}}` or `A:{1.2:B}`?
I use such paths often when I write tools, so I wanted a language that prevents me from doing crazy stuff.

## Strings

sdin has four string syntaxes, each for a different use case, just as a hardware store has fifty kinds of screws, each for a different use case.

Literal strings with single quotes represent strings verbatim but cannot contain single quotes or newlines.
They are recommended for strings written by humans:

```
LiteralString: 'string value'
```

``https://go.dev/ref/spec#String_literals|Go-style quoted strings`` can encode anything with escaping.
They are recommended for strings generated by code:

```
QuotedString: "string with newline:\nand with double quotes: \""
```

Raw strings are in the format of ``|'''tag|content'''``.
The content of the string is the raw bytes between the pipe symbol and the closing quotes.
The number of quotes can increase if the content contains multiple single quotes.
`tag` is an arbitrary string (no newlines or pipe symbol though) for adding metadata to the string.

```
File: {
	Content: '''go|package main

func main() {
	return
}
'''
}

TaglessRawString: '''|The tag is optional but the pipe isn't.'''
```

Multiline strings are in the format of ``|'''tag↲...'''`` where ↲ means newline.
The formatter maintains the indentation of this string based on the indent of the closing quotes, so the closing quotes must be on their own line.
The value of the string is the content between the first newline and the closing quotes with the indentation removed, so the resulting string always ends with a newline.
This is the recommended format for most multiline strings; use raw strings for anything this format can't express nicely.

````split
# Before formatter:
File: {
	Content: '''go
package main

func main() {
	return
}
'''
}

EmbeddedQuotes: '''''
			Use five or more quotes if the text has '''' in it.
			'''''
---
# After formatter:
File: {
	Content: '''go
		package main

		func main() {
			return
		}
		'''
}

EmbeddedQuotes: '''''
	Use five or more quotes if the text has '''' in it.
	'''''
````

Tags have no meaning to sdin itself, but tools can use them.
For example, the formatter recognizes `sdin`-tagged strings and reformats their content as sdin:

```split
# Before formatter:
InnerSDIN:'''sdin
Sentence: {
Greeting:'Hello'
Target:'World'
}
'''
---
# After formatter:
InnerSDIN: '''sdin
	Sentence: {
		Greeting: 'Hello'
		Target:   'World'
	}
	'''
```

The content of the other strings is left as is: the formatter doesn't run `go fmt` on `go`-tagged strings.

## Forms

sdin supports `{...}` structs and `[...]` lists just like JSON.
However, sdin also supports `tagname(...)` forms.
They're basically a mix of structs and lists: you can have both named values and nameless values in them.
Names are the tokens that end with a colon; they are the field names in `{...}` structs, while in `[...]` lists all values are nameless.

!html <img src=sdin-forms.avif class="cCenter" alt="what-the-hell-is-this meme">

You can use them where you would need sum types:

```
Alert: {
	Name:    HighLatency
	Expr:    'p99 > 500ms'
	Notify+: email('oncall@example.com' Subject:'p99 latency spike')
	Notify+: slack('#alerts' Mention:@oncall)
	Notify+: pagerduty(Service:web Severity:critical)
}
```

Note that `HighLatency`, `@oncall`, `web`, and `critical` are all "bare tokens" (not strings) that consist only of non-reserved symbols (no spaces, comment markers, parens, etc.).
More on this in the tokens section.

For reference, in JSON, this would look visually noisier:

```
"Alert": {
	"Name": "HighLatency",
	"Expr": "p99 > 500ms",
	"Notify": [
		{"Type":"email", "To":"oncall@example.com", "Subject":"p99 latency spike"},
		{"Type":"slack", "Channel":"#alerts", "Mention":"@oncall"},
		{"Type":"pagerduty", "Service":"web", "Severity":"critical"}
	]
}
```

Forms can nest just like structs and lists:

```
Backup: {
	Path:     '/var/lib/db'
	Schedule: cron('0 3 * * *')
	Retain:   max(days(30) count(10))
	Target:   s3('backups/db' Region:eu-central-1 Encrypt:kms(Alias:'backup-key'))
}
```

Forms don't automatically convert to ordinary types.
You need to work with the AST to process them.
In exchange, the config structure remains simple and easy to refactor while all logic is in high-level code.
See the ``#ProcessingForms2|Processing forms 2`` section for example code that converts the Alert example to JSON.

The field names in forms are no longer restricted to identifiers; they can be any bare token because the flat syntax doesn't cross forms.
This is valid:

```
BuildCop: OndutyConfiguration(
	Aliases(
		alice@: 'Alice Alicious'
		bob@:   'Bob Bobicious'
	)
	Schedule(
		2026-10-19.0600: alice@
		2026-10-26.0600: bob@
	)
)
```

Forms turn out to be quite expressive.
Part of the @/sdinthrough challenge is to implement various DSLs.
One example in the challenge is encoding sdin schemas:

```
Date: struct({
	Year:  int(checks.IntRange(1900 2100))
	Month: identifier(checks.MatchToken('Jan|Feb|Mar|Apr|May|Jun|Jul|Aug|Sep|Oct|Nov|Dec'))
	Day:   int(checks.IntRange(1 31))
})

# const(typename value) defines a constant.
# Errors out if `value` doesn't satisfy `typename`'s schema.
ReferenceDay: const(Date {Year:2006 Month:Jan Day:2})
```

Fair warning: if you overuse forms, your config will start looking like Lisp, and your data might stop being simple configuration.

## Directives

Directives are `#%` comments that tools can interpret specially.
Each `#%` line must be a valid sdin form value.
One example is the formatter's directive to keep a list sorted:

```split
# Before formatter:
#% sdin.KeepSorted()
UserList: [
	charlie
	alice
	dave
	frank
	bob
]
---
# After formatter:
#% sdin.KeepSorted()
UserList: [
	alice
	bob
	charlie
	dave
	frank
]
```

Another example was in the ``#FullExample|#FullExample`` section: it used `stdemo.SkipJSONEmit()`, which indicated that the field should be stripped when converting to JSON.
`stdemo.SkipJSONEmit()` is not an sdin feature; rather, it's something your tool can easily implement.

