## Tokens 1: The Bytes type example

sdin takes a somewhat unorthodox approach to bare values:

```
SimpleNumber:        123
NumberLikeToken:     123_456
Enums:               [January February May]
Boolean:             true
Requirements.Memory: 100MB
Requirements.Disk:   unlimited
```

A bare token can be anything consisting of letters, numbers, and symbols (except whitespace, colons, braces, etc.).
sdin doesn't define what these values represent.
The types into which the tokens are parsed define the meaning of the tokens.

!html <img src=sdin-tokens.png class="cInvertibleImage cCenter" alt="a 4-panel comic about the absurdity of sdin's token system">

Suppose you want to ensure that byte-limit fields like Memory or Disk are either `unlimited` or a number that ends in `B` as in `1024B` or `1KB`.
You would support this with a custom `Bytes` type that implements the FromSDINToken and IntoSDINToken methods:

```
type Bytes int64

func (v *Bytes) FromSDINToken(t sdin.Token) error {
	...
}

func (v *Bytes) IntoSDINToken() sdin.Token {
	...
}

type Requirements struct {
	Memory Bytes
	Disk   Bytes
}

func main() {
	r, err := ³sdin.Unmarshal[Requirements](`Memory:8GB Disk:unlimited`)³
	if err != nil {
		fmt.Printf("UnmarshalFailed: %v\n", err)
		os.Exit(1)
	}

	fmt.Printf("As requirements struct: %+v\n", r)
	fmt.Printf("As sdin again:          %s\n", sdin.NewNode("", &r))
	fmt.Println(sdin.Unmarshal[Requirements](`Memory:1024`))
	fmt.Println(sdin.Unmarshal[Requirements](`Memory:true`))
	fmt.Println(sdin.Unmarshal[Requirements](`Disk:'unlimited'`))

	// Output
	// As requirements struct: ³{Memory:8000000000 Disk:-1}³
	// As sdin again:          ³{Memory:8GB Disk:unlimited}³
	// {0 0} sdin.IntoForUnmarshal: sdin.TokenInto line=1 path=Memory: sdin.FromSDINToken type=*main.Bytes: InvalidBytesToken token="1024": must be an integer with a B suffix
	// {0 0} sdin.IntoForUnmarshal: sdin.TokenInto line=1 path=Memory: sdin.FromSDINToken type=*main.Bytes: InvalidBytesToken token="true": must be an integer with a B suffix
	// {0 0} sdin.IntoForUnmarshal: sdin.TokenInto line=1 path=Disk: sdin.FromSDINToken type=*main.Bytes: InvalidBytesToken token="'unlimited'": must be an integer with a B suffix
}
```

Notice how the Bytes type is just an int64, yet the sdin package can smoothly convert the `unlimited` token to the -1 number, while returning an error for unexpected tokens.
The function for that is this:

```
func (v *Bytes) FromSDINToken(t sdin.Token) error {
	if t.Repr() == "unlimited" {
		*v = -1
		return nil
	}
	suffixes := []string{"TB", "GB", "MB", "KB", "B"}
	multipliers := []int64{1e12, 1e9, 1e6, 1e3, 1}
	for i, sf := range suffixes {
		if s, ok := strings.CutSuffix(t.Repr(), sf); ok {
			num, err := strconv.ParseInt(s, 10, 64)
			if err != nil {
				return fmt.Errorf("ParseInteger token=%q: %v", t.Repr(), err)
			}
			*v = Bytes(num * multipliers[i])
			return nil
		}
	}
	return fmt.Errorf("InvalidBytesToken token=%q: must be an integer with a B suffix", t.Repr())
}
```

The function receives `sdin.Token`, which is just a `string` of the bytes as they appeared in the input (hah, the name Structured Data ``em|Input`` Notation does seem fitting after all).
`sdin.Token` has a bunch of helper methods, but you can get the raw string with `.Repr()`, and parse it with ordinary code.
The above function also enforces that the string must end with `B`, and computes the correct byte amount based on the suffix.

Converting from a `Bytes` number is similarly straightforward:

```
func (v *Bytes) IntoSDINToken() sdin.Token {
	if *v < 0 {
		return sdin.MakeTokenFromRepr("unlimited")
	}
	if *v == 0 {
		return sdin.MakeTokenFromRepr("0B")
	}
	suffixes := []string{"TB", "GB", "MB", "KB", "B"}
	multipliers := []int64{1e12, 1e9, 1e6, 1e3, 1}
	for i, m := range multipliers {
		if int64(*v)%m == 0 {
			return sdin.MakeTokenFromRepr(strconv.FormatInt(int64(*v)/m, 10) + suffixes[i])
		}
	}
	return sdin.MakeTokenFromRepr("BytesUnreachableCode")
}
```

There's a big caveat: for code simplicity reasons, sdin assumes that all values have a valid sdin representation.
So this function cannot return an error.
It's up to the type's owner to ensure sdin's marshaller is never called on uninitialized or invalid values.
This allows `sdin.NewNode()` to not return an error, which then makes building an sdin node tree simple.
It also makes printing values for debugging easy, as seen in this snippet from above:

```
	fmt.Printf("As sdin again:          %s\n", sdin.NewNode("", &r))
```

This has one serious ramification: sdin's portability is limited.
The above bytes tokens can only be parsed in environments where you implemented the bytes-parsing code.

## Tokens 2: Builtin types example

My sdin Go module supports most builtin types such as integers, floats, booleans, and dates.
For integers, it supports some well-known suffixes too.
To make digit grouping easier, it ignores the underscore symbol in these numeric tokens.

```
$ cat types.sdin
SimpleNumber:       123
DigitGroupedNumber: 123_456_789
Bytes:              10Ki
Length:             10M
Hex:                0xdead_BEEF
Float:              1e6

String:     "hello-string"
Identifier: HelloIdentifier
BareToken:  hello-~!@$%^&*+,./;-symbols

Bool:     true
Duration: 1.5m
Date:     2006-01-02

# Value tokens cannot contain : because that's a reserved symbol.
# But you can use underscore instead to keep the time part readable.
# Time is UTC by default but you can customize it with the `@` specification.
Time:          2006-01-02.15_04_05
LocalTime:     2006-01-02.15_04_05@local
TimezonedTime: 2006-01-02.15_04_05@Asia/Tokyo
```

All this can be parsed like this:

```
type Types struct {
	SimpleNumber       int
	DigitGroupedNumber int
	Bytes              int
	Length             int
	Hex                int
	Float              float64

	String     string
	Identifier sdin.Identifier
	BareToken  sdin.BareToken

	Bool     bool
	Duration time.Duration
	Date     time.Time

	Time          time.Time
	LocalTime     time.Time
	TimezonedTime time.Time
}

func main() {
	data, err := os.ReadFile("types.sdin")
⁰	if err != nil { ... }
	types, err := sdin.Unmarshal[Types](string(data))
⁰	if err != nil { ... }
	s, err := json.MarshalIndent(types, "", "\t")
⁰	if err != nil { ... }
	fmt.Printf("%s\n", s)
}

// Output:
{
	"SimpleNumber": 123,
	"DigitGroupedNumber": 123456789,
	"Bytes": 10240,
	"Length": 10000000,
	"Hex": 3735928559,
	"Float": 1000000,
	"String": "hello-string",
	"Identifier": "HelloIdentifier",
	"BareToken": "hello-~!@$%^\u0026*+,./;-symbols",
	"Bool": true,
	"Duration": 90000000000,
	"Date": "2006-01-02T00:00:00Z",
	"Time": "2006-01-02T15:04:05Z",
	"LocalTime": "2006-01-02T15:04:05+01:00",
	"TimezonedTime": "2006-01-02T15:04:05+09:00"
}
```

At the same time, sdin will reject obviously bad pairings:

```
root, err := sdin.Unmarshal[*sdin.Node](string(data))
⁰if err != nil { ... }
if _, err := root.Get("SimpleNumber").As[bool](); err != nil {
	fmt.Printf("ParseNumberAsBool: %v\n", err)
}
if _, err := root.Get("Identifier").As[string](); err != nil {
	fmt.Printf("ParseIdentifierAsString: %v\n", err)
}
if _, err := root.Get("String").As[sdin.Identifier](); err != nil {
	fmt.Printf("ParseStringAsIdentifier: %v\n", err)
}
if _, err := root.Get("BareToken").As[sdin.Identifier](); err != nil {
	fmt.Printf("ParseBareTokenAsIdentifier: %v\n", err)
}
if _, err := root.Get("SimpleNumber").As[time.Time](); err != nil {
	fmt.Printf("ParseNumberAsTime: %v\n", err)
}

// Output:
ParseNumberAsBool: sdin.TokenInto line=1: sdin.NonBoolToken token="123": bools must be either true or false
ParseIdentifierAsString: sdin.TokenInto line=9: sdin.ParseStringToken: sdin.ParseBareAsString s="HelloIdentifier": bare tokens are not considered as strings
ParseStringAsIdentifier: sdin.TokenInto line=8: sdin.StringAsIdentifier s="\"hello-string\"": strings are not considered identifiers
ParseBareTokenAsIdentifier: sdin.TokenInto line=10: sdin.NonIdentifierCharacter i=5 ch='-' s="hello-~!@$%^&*+,./;-symbols": identifier must contain only letters, numbers, or underscores
ParseNumberAsTime: sdin.TokenInto line=1: sdin.NotADateTime s="123"
```

As the ParseIdentifierAsString error shows, sdin doesn't suffer from the ``https://langdev.stackexchange.com/questions/1123|Norway problem``.
It can clearly distinguish enums from string values.
But you can certainly implement the problem for your custom types if you like footguns.

You can also track field presence if you use pointers (not needed for lists and maps though):

```
