## Nodes 1: List sorting example

The Go module strives to make it easy to manipulate the sdin AST structure.
Rather than providing getters/setters, the `Node` struct exposes the full AST structure with no hidden fields:

```
$ go doc sdin.Node
package sdin // import "github.com/ypsu/sdin"

type Node struct {
	// List of comments and newline tokens that appear before the value.
	Pre []string

	// Name, Value, and Post tokens are all defined on a single line.
	// In structs, Name is an identifier which matches the regex `[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z0-9_]*`.
	// In forms, Name can be any bare token.
	Name  string // the bare token before the colon, or empty if this is an unnamed node (e.g. in lists)
	Value Token  // the value token; can be `[`, `{`, or `tagname(` for composite nodes (`:[`, `:{`, `:(` for the flat versions)
	Post  string // the newline or comment, if any, after the value (or opening delimiter in composite nodes)

	// The 1-based line on which the Value token was defined.
	Line int

	// Fields relevant only for struct/list/form nodes follow.
	Children []*Node

	// List of comments and newline tokens that appear before the matching }]) token for {[( values.
	PreClose []string

	// For {[( values this token appears after the matching }]) token.
	// Can be a comment for a sidecomment, a newline to indicate EOL, or empty to keep the object on a single line.
	PostClose string
}
    Node represents an sdin value.

func NewFlatNode(name string, value any) *Node
func NewNode(name string, value any) *Node
func Parse(kind Token, p string) (*Node, error)
func (n *Node) As[T any]() (T, error)
func (n *Node) Directives() []*Node
func (n *Node) FormFields() *Node
func (n *Node) FormValues() *Node
func (n *Node) Format() string
func (n *Node) Get(path string) *Node
func (n *Node) Kind() Kind
func (n *Node) MarshalJSON() ([]byte, error)
func (n *Node) Replace(fn func(n *Node) (*Node, error)) error
func (n *Node) String() string
func (n *Node) UnmarshalJSON(p []byte) error
```

Using sdin requires a good understanding of this structure, but in exchange it allows writing succinct, idiomatic code.

Suppose you have the following sdin structure that defines groups and their members:

```
$ cat groups.sdin
employees: {
	Description: "This group lists all the employees."
	Data: {
		Members: ['charlie' 'alice' 'bob' 'frank' 'dave']
	}
}

developers: {
	Description: '''
		This group lists all the developers.
		They have write access to the code repository.
		'''
	Data.Members: [
		'charlie'
		# Frank is both a developer and sysadmin.
		'frank'
		'alice'
	]
}

sysadmins: {
	Description: ''''|This group lists all the employees with production access.''''

	Data.Members+: 'bob'
	Data.Members+: 'frank'  # Frank is both a developer and sysadmin.
	Data.Members+: 'dave'
}
```

You want to write a tool that sorts the member lists.
The full code for that is only this:

```
func main() {
	data, err := os.ReadFile("groups.sdin")
⁰	if err != nil { ... }
	root, err := sdin.Unmarshal[*sdin.Node](string(data))
⁰	if err != nil { ... }
	for _, g := range root.Children {
		if members := g.Get("Data.Members"); members != nil {
			slices.SortFunc(members.Children, func(a, b *sdin.Node) int {
				astr, _ := a.As[string]()
				bstr, _ := b.As[string]()
				return cmp.Compare(astr, bstr)
			})
		}
	}
	fmt.Print(root.Format())
}
```

Here's the output of that.
Notice how it kept the newlines / flatness / comments intact:

```
employees: {
	Description: "This group lists all the employees."
	Data: {
		Members: ['alice' 'bob' 'charlie' 'dave' 'frank']
	}
}

developers: {
	Description: '''
		This group lists all the developers.
		They have write access to the code repository.
		'''
	Data.Members: [
		'alice'
		'charlie'
